premise environment.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:16.760
So moving to the
left, what do we have?

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:17.779
Google Apigee.

00:04:17.779 --> 00:04:20.300
I want to have RESTful
API interfaces.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:23.720
That innovation team that's
working is familiar with REST.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.390
Your mainframe or some of
your legacy applications

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:28.820
may not actually
talk RESTful API.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:31.571
Even if they do, perhaps
there are some other nuances

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:34.070
we'll get into in a minute when
we talk about that use case.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:37.220
So I have those existing
services on premise.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:39.560
Maybe I want to run
Kubernetes on premise.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:42.770
My security officer, my
application, data gravity,

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:44.210
I need something on premise.

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:45.980
But I still want it
to be Kubernetes.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.160
But I want it to be
production ready.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.880
What do I want to
run it on top of?

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:52.790
Cisco sells hyper converged
and our traditional converged

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:55.100
infrastructure, so
HyperFlex and also software

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.174
defined networking.

00:04:57.174 --> 00:04:58.590
So a little bit
more specifically,

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.006
what are we doing
in the products?

00:05:00.006 --> 00:05:01.820
So on Google Cloud,
we are going to be

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:06.470
offering in August the Cisco CSR
1000v Virtual Router on Google

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:10.280
Cloud to connect that consistent
connectivity between on premise

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:10.940
and Cloud.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.470
This is Cisco's
kind of heritage.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.870
We obviously have lots of
other products besides.

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:18.530
So it could be firewalls,
it could be physical ASRs,

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:21.530
CSI, ISR routers.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:24.170
It could also be that you have
a Cisco device on one side

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.540
and you want to connect a
Google Cloud VPN on the other,

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.106
also supported
the configuration.

00:05:28.106 --> 00:05:30.230
Or you could be using a
connectivity partner that's

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:33.439
using low latency, high
throughput Google Cloud

00:05:33.439 --> 00:05:33.980
Interconnect.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:36.260
So those are all
supported scenarios.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:38.460
I want a consistent
security experience, so

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:41.390
combination of firewalls
and defense and depth.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.679
That isn't enough
in a Cloud world.

00:05:43.679 --> 00:05:45.470
We can do things like
intrusion prevention,

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.740
but what happens when half the
traffic goes off to the Cloud?

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.450
So things like
StealthWatch Cloud.

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:53.900
We have sensors on
premise, sensors on GKE,

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:56.922
and also VPC flow logs so that
we have a full visibility,

00:05:56.922 --> 00:05:58.713
because we have an
extended trust model now

00:05:58.713 --> 00:05:59.740
on the security.

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:02.120
And then coming down onto
the Cisco container platform,

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:05.570
having the option to run Istio
either on Cisco container

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:08.876
platform or GKE, but
also Istio's promise--

00:06:08.876 --> 00:06:10.250
you saw some demos
in the keynote

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:12.780
showing its automatic
visibility of both sides.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.780
So Cisco's been working
very closely with Google

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:17.562
to get that multi-Cloud,
multi-site capability

00:06:17.562 --> 00:06:19.770
integrated into Istio because
that's important to us,

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.610
especially.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:23.995
Contiv is our container
networking technology

00:06:23.995 --> 00:06:28.090
that then sits on top
of the physical assets.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:29.440
So couple of use cases.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:30.670
We'll get very specific now.

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:33.100
I mentioned Apigee on premise.

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:36.250
So Google Apigee is also sold
as a software as a service

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:39.080
offering, but maybe you want it
on premise for various reasons.

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:41.920
So we, in the solution,
are supporting deploying

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:43.514
Apigee Edge on premise.

00:06:43.514 --> 00:06:44.680
And why would you need that?

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:47.002
Maybe your application already
does have RESTful APIs.

00:06:47.002 --> 00:06:47.710
Maybe it doesn't.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:50.410
So first and foremost,
giving a RESTful interface

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:52.630
on premise to things that
might completely live out

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:56.200
in the Cloud, your mainframe,
your physical database assets.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:59.680
But more importantly, it also
can do telemetry, control,

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.389
who should be able to
access it, reporting.

00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:03.430
Maybe you want to charge.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:05.430
The data you have on
premise is valuable to you.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:07.870
Apigee can create a front
end so that you can actually

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:09.953
charge someone every time
they pull from your API,

00:07:09.953 --> 00:07:11.980
so start to monetize
your data on premise.

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:14.080
And maybe that's your
service all in itself

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:15.880
is getting something
out past your firewall

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:19.880
in a very controlled RESTful API
way and making money from it.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:21.370
So a second use case.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:23.290
I have an application
on premise I want

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:25.347
to extend out into the Cloud.

00:07:25.347 --> 00:07:27.180
The couple of technical
sessions I mentioned

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.140
that are going to follow up are
going to actually demo this use

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:30.700
case very specifically.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:33.160
But say, for example, you
have an application on premise

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:36.160
and your customers can
put in feedback on it.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.499
Wouldn't it be nice to do
customer sentiment analytics?

00:07:38.499 --> 00:07:40.540
Do you want to own that
and operate that service?

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:41.535
Perhaps not.

00:07:41.535 --> 00:07:42.910
You can go ahead
and use services

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:45.980
like Pub/Sub, Cloud Span,
or BigQuery, Big Tables,

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:47.236
et cetera from Google.

00:07:47.236 --> 00:07:48.610
And so the on
premise application

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:51.310
gets extensibility
into the public Cloud

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:55.960
to get those additional platform
services through open service

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:57.400
broker and service
catalog, which

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.920
are built into the on premise
Cisco container platform.

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:05.130
Lastly, the most common use
case we hear from customers.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.950
Maybe you want dev on
public Cloud, Google Cloud,

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:12.120
and you want dev
on private Cloud.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:14.250
Really, it's a choice,
customers are choosing

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:15.607
to run things in various ways.

00:08:15.607 --> 00:08:17.690
One team may operate very
differently than another

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:18.190
as well.

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:19.870
One size doesn't fit all.

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:21.990
So I want to have a
100% upstream Kubernetes

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:22.990
environment.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:24.930
I mentioned the innovation team.

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:28.170
So your innovation team
also was off in a corner

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:30.330
running Kubernetes
on their laptop,

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:33.539
writing services, re-platforming
your applications,

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:36.059
and creating this new
thing that the executives

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:38.280
asked for and envisioned.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:40.919
Who takes the phone
call Friday night at 2

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:43.837
AM when the Kubernetes
application doesn't work?

00:08:43.837 --> 00:08:46.170
How many of you guys are all
production ops, IT ops kind

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:47.460
of folks, and not developers?

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:48.001
Raised hands.

00:08:48.001 --> 00:08:50.440
Who's not developers
in the room?

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:51.820
There's a lot of developers.

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:52.320
All right.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.660
So I'm talking to
you IT ops guys.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:57.600
You know that you take the call
at 2:00 AM when it's broken.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.190
So some of you may not even
be familiar with Kubernetes.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.035
It's the world that
the developers are in,

00:09:02.035 --> 00:09:04.410
they're platforming, they're
testing, they're developing,

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.159
they're pushing code,
they're doing things

00:09:06.159 --> 00:09:08.850
like Agilent and CICD,
continuous integration

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.320
continuous deployment.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.140
And then they come
to you and say I'd

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:14.529
like to take this production.

00:09:14.529 --> 00:09:16.320
And you start asking
the obvious questions.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:17.820
Is it secure?

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.290
Does it scale?

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:20.880
Is it documented?

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:22.110
Who needs access to it?

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:24.900
All the things that developers
don't necessarily care about.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:28.200
Security, developers
usually deliver outcomes.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:31.320
Security oftentimes isn't
built into the application.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:33.810
I think we're starting to see
that change with regulations

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:34.934
like GDPR.

00:09:34.934 --> 00:09:37.350
We're starting to have to build
more into the application.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.990
But we still need also
that umbrella of security

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:41.406
that covers everything.

00:09:41.406 --> 00:09:43.530
So really, this use case,
and what we're working on

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.330
is you need to be
able to deploy.

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:48.090
The developers need to have
something they can depend on.

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:49.710
It needs be production ready.

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:51.250
And who do you
have to support you

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:53.220
when it's broken in the
middle of the night?

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:55.510
So this is a lot of
open source technology.

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:57.312
So we're wrapping
this with support.

00:09:57.312 --> 00:09:58.770
So really, that's
the biggest thing

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.140
I think that Google and
Cisco have been working on,

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:04.470
mostly, is Cisco has
an enterprise class

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:05.827
technical support capability.

00:10:05.827 --> 00:10:07.410
You may already have
a lot of products

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:10.200
from Cisco, routers, switches,
firewalls, other software

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.000
products, and you
know you can call

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.670
Cisco 24/7 around
the clock and you'll

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.400
get someone who will help
you and prioritize it and get

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:18.400
it fixed for you.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:19.983
So that's what we're
bringing to this.

00:10:19.983 --> 00:10:21.990
We also, obviously,
have a partner ecosystem

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.650
that supports this and
can design and install it.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:27.519
So I mentioned secure.

00:10:27.519 --> 00:10:30.060
We need to start doing things
like proactive security, threat

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:30.720
detection.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:33.390
Integrated VPN and connectivity
is one bit, but also

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:34.580
behavioral analysis.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:37.690
I'm going to talk specifically
now about StealthWatch Cloud.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:39.190
So those of you who
haven't seen it,

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:41.430
this is kind of a StealthWatch
Cloud user interface.

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:42.670
I come into a dashboard.

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:44.491
This is a SaaS
offering from Cisco.

00:10:44.491 --> 00:10:46.740
So you don't have to worry
about running the platform.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:49.710
You just deploy the sensors
and consume the intelligence

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:51.630
analytics that the
platform provides.

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:54.540
I see that I have a number
of sensors deployed.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:57.750
So this happened to be from
Cisco Live US last month.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:01.177
I have different kind
of workers deployed.

00:11:01.177 --> 00:11:02.760
I have a Google
Kubernetes environment

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.380
I have sensors deployed to.

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:05.654
I have some VM ware.

00:11:05.654 --> 00:11:08.070
How many of you think that
your container applications are

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:10.110
never going to talk
to anything that's

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:12.889
your old applications, your
virtuals, your physical?

00:11:12.889 --> 00:11:13.680
I don't think that.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:14.860
I think for a while
we're actually

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:16.680
going to be running
in this composite mode

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.780
where the new
applications need to talk

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:20.430
to the other
applications that haven't

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.380
been re-plaformed
and modernized yet.

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:24.150
So we have our existing
data center assets

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.880
we need to start getting
technical and security

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:28.090
telemetry from.

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:30.180
And then finally,
Google Cloud Platform

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:32.190
has VPC flow log
support now that

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:33.731
has come out and been released.

00:11:33.731 --> 00:11:35.730
And so we have the ability
to see the flow logs.

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:38.030
The data, you can't see
underneath the Cloud

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:40.800
is exposed to the flow
logs, and StealthWatch Cloud

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.100
picks that up.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:43.200
So what does that mean?

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.940
I get visualization.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:46.410
Who's talking to who?

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:47.360
How are they talking?

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:49.318
What kind of alerts, what
kind of connectivity?

00:11:49.318 --> 00:11:51.090
How many observations
are we making?

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:53.820
Looking at networks, talking
to what kind of traffic

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:56.280
are they exchanging?

00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:58.320
How many connections are active?

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:01.150
Looking at the heuristics
and past performance--

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:03.702
the last 24 hours I've had
this kind of traffic pattern--

00:12:03.702 --> 00:12:05.410
and you can see there's
check boxes where

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:08.940
maybe events have occurred
that you want to click into.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:10.380
So important thing.

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:12.870
Difference from a firewall, or
like an intrusion prevention

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:16.260
system, the models are taking
all these various inputs

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:17.550
we just described.

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:20.040
They're then correlating
it with entity behavior.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:22.890
A firewall may say
this country can never

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:25.420
talk to me because I don't
do business in that country.

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:30.924
Or I have a pattern match, and
intrusion prevention oftentimes

00:12:30.924 --> 00:12:33.590
looks at a pattern, and you tend
to get a lot of false positives

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:35.930
sometimes as that
pattern changes.

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:38.120
But wouldn't you like
to know, every day

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:40.250
I get these kind of
connections, and I then

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:41.836
send the connection
off to Google,

00:12:41.836 --> 00:12:43.460
and the response back
from Google Cloud

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:46.970
is normally one packet that's
20 kilobytes of an answer.

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:48.620
What happens if that
behavior changed?

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:50.703
All of a sudden I have a
million packets going out

00:12:50.703 --> 00:12:53.420
to Google Cloud and the
response is now 40k or 100k.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:54.740
The behavior changed.

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:56.240
So using kind of a
heuristic pattern

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.340
matching and the
behavior analysis,

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:01.070
we're looking at the security
to say something's different.

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:02.840
So this isn't just
simply firewalls

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:04.940
and intrusion prevention.

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:06.140
And what do you kind of get?

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:09.440
Now I actually have
very actionable alerts.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:12.680
Hey, people who don't
normally access this system

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:16.850
have tried to do SSH
into your GKE clusters.

00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:17.499
Drill into it.

00:13:17.499 --> 00:13:19.790
It's been accessed from a
country that doesn't normally

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:20.120
exist.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:21.828
Maybe this was a change
in your business.

00:13:21.828 --> 00:13:24.259
You'll be able to quickly go
and accept this or mark this

00:13:24.259 --> 00:13:25.550
as something that's actionable.

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:27.410
If it's something that's
normal, your business patterns

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:29.780
have changed, your security
ops folks click this

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:31.520
and say this is normal.

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:35.130
The behavior no longer
recognizes this as an anomaly.

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:36.590
So we have lots of information.

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:38.840
The countries will load
in with the IP addresses.

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:40.790
Everything is applicable
in here to drill in.

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:41.580
What kind of endpoints?

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:43.570
What kind of activity do we
have going through the system?

00:13:43.570 --> 00:13:45.236
So this shows you
behavior of endpoints,

00:13:45.236 --> 00:13:47.240
and when your peak times
in your business are.

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:48.950
So this is an
example of the inputs

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:51.630
that are used in the model
and the behavior analysis.

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:54.230
What kind of traffic do we
see, what kind of ins and outs?

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:55.160
How many connections?

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.446
Internal devices versus
extrernal devices?

00:13:57.446 --> 00:13:58.820
So it's graphing
all that for you

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:01.220
and showing what's occurring.

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:06.650
Now how many are very familiar
with Kubernetes in this room?

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:08.170
Again, about half, 2/3.

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:09.620
All right.

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:11.120
So how many of you
prefer the CLI?

