00:17:06.359 --> 00:17:10.319
And we're going to run into
our cloud management platform

00:17:10.319 --> 00:17:11.730
product.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:13.530
So when the developers
were deploying this,

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:15.329
they may have
deployed this using

00:17:15.329 --> 00:17:18.030
YAML files, text
files that describe

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:20.670
what the deployment looks like,
how the configuration should

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:22.670
work.

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:26.824
In Cisco, we also have a visual
cloud management product.

00:17:26.824 --> 00:17:27.740
So let me describe it.

00:17:27.740 --> 00:17:28.210
A couple of things.

00:17:28.210 --> 00:17:29.668
The Thursday session
is going to go

00:17:29.668 --> 00:17:32.600
under a lot more detail on both
Container Platform and Cloud

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:33.260
Center.

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:37.280
But what I have here is a drag
and drop topology capability.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:39.380
Maybe, for example,
your innovation team

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:42.530
is really good at deploying
their specific application.

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:44.030
But you have another
team that maybe

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:46.880
is the experts in
Cassandra, or your NoSQL,

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:50.780
or your relational database, or
your production load balancers.

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:53.240
So one team may not
have the whole set

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:56.480
of ingredients it takes to
make the system production.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:18:00.890
Therefore, we have, on the left,
all the various components,

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:03.350
and we're capturing the
organizational best practices

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:06.050
and automation of all
the best components.

00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:07.280
Think of these like widgets.

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:09.530
You may want a blue car,
you may want a red plane.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:10.820
We might share some widgets.

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:12.251
On the left is all the widgets.

00:18:12.251 --> 00:18:14.000
In the middle is where
we're dragging them

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:16.370
into the outcome, the
blue car or the red plane.

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:18.260
So this is my socks application.

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:21.360
Lots of little micro
services, containers.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:22.700
This also could mix in.

00:18:22.700 --> 00:18:25.370
Maybe I need to put
in a very traditional

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:27.320
virtual machine-based
message bus,

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:29.950
or I want to drag out
an operating system

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:31.610
because I need a jump
box or something.

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:34.370
Or I need to drag out
an NFS file system.

00:18:34.370 --> 00:18:36.470
So the drag and drop
capability environment

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:38.270
means that people
who don't necessarily

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:41.090
know as much about
these individual widgets

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:45.260
may still be able to get the
expert organizational knowledge

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:47.450
and add it to their outcome.

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:49.600
So now I have something
that's reusable.

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:52.520
And so when we talk about I
want to be able to deploy this,

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:55.864
dev wants this on
Google Cloud, prod

00:18:55.864 --> 00:18:58.280
wants this on the internal
container platform environment,

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:00.000
or vice versa.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:04.370
So what we have in CLI and YAML
files is technically accurate,

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:06.069
but it may not also
have guardrails.

00:19:06.069 --> 00:19:07.610
So this system also
gives the ability

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:10.190
to have tag-based governance.

00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:12.410
Maybe devs don't know
prod should go here

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:14.929
and dev should go there,
or stage also goes here.

00:19:14.929 --> 00:19:17.220
Or maybe they don't know that
data classification needs

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:20.436
to go over an encrypted
path, and non-PCI maybe

00:19:20.436 --> 00:19:21.560
doesn't have to go over it.

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:24.260
So those are the kind of things,
the non-technical guardrails,

00:19:24.260 --> 00:19:27.000
if you will, that a Cloud
management platform adds

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:30.080
into the technical
capability the innovation

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:31.860
team's delivering.

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:35.370
So when I come and look
at these deployments,

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:38.040
the user can now self-serve,
see the number of deployments.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:39.436
I have a number of things here.

00:19:39.436 --> 00:19:40.560
I have an Istio deployment.

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:43.740
My socks application that
I showed you earlier,

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:45.024
I've deployed it.

00:19:45.024 --> 00:19:46.440
So the developer
can actually see.

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:47.460
Or this could be the admin.

