this is what the Cisco
Technical Assistance Center,

00:20:34.434 --> 00:20:35.940
working with Google,
will actually

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:37.980
help you fix when it's broken.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:39.502
So all the services
and visibility

00:20:39.502 --> 00:20:41.460
is exposed to everyone,
not just the innovation

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:43.626
team that happens to be the
container and Kubernetes

00:20:43.626 --> 00:20:44.772
experts.

00:20:44.772 --> 00:20:46.980
And ultimately we get it
all in a nice dashboard that

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:49.620
shows us what's it costing us,
how much are we running, how

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:51.792
often, where all of the
different various clouds

00:20:51.792 --> 00:20:52.750
we're using, et cetera.

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:58.787
So let's talk a little bit
about how this works in practice

00:20:58.787 --> 00:21:00.370
and some of the other
problems we have

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:03.220
with enterprise applications.

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:04.690
So over the last
couple of months,

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:07.810
Google and Cisco and SAP have
been working very closely

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:08.540
together.

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:10.690
So SAP is launching Data Hub.

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:14.630
Many of you may
be SAP HANA users.

00:21:14.630 --> 00:21:16.590
They just had their SFP
SAPPHIRE Conference.

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:19.960
So they've been announcing
Data Hub and their HANA data

00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:21.160
management platform.

00:21:21.160 --> 00:21:24.340
This is a great example of
an enterprise application.

00:21:24.340 --> 00:21:26.170
So Cisco and Google
are also working

00:21:26.170 --> 00:21:29.770
with these types of
enterprise providers

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:36.160
because they're modernizing
their platforms and software

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:40.130
to use Cloud native and
container concepts as well.

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:42.010
So SAP Data Hub has
been completely built

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:43.586
on top of Kubernetes.

00:21:43.586 --> 00:21:45.460
Now I'm not going to go
into too much detail.

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:48.130
I think SAP is here and has
a session this week as well.

00:21:48.130 --> 00:21:51.340
But really, we're describing
a hybrid environment now.

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:53.590
I have a data gravity
problem oftentimes.

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:57.040
Data Hub is very specifically
looking at AI and ML,

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:59.830
streams and databases,
apps with big data,

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:01.270
object stores and IOT devices.

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:03.636
Those may all be running
out in Google Cloud

00:22:03.636 --> 00:22:05.260
they may want to
bring all of that back

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:08.410
and coordinate it with ERP data,
customer data, sales data, et.

00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:11.680
Cetera so SAP Data
Hub has components

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:15.010
that are containerized that can
run both on GKE and on Cisco

00:22:15.010 --> 00:22:16.540
container platform on premise.

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:17.890
Depends on where your data is.

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:19.872
Some customers may have
more data on premise

00:22:19.872 --> 00:22:21.580
that they're actually
trying to ship out.

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:22.750
Other customers may
have a lot that's

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:24.070
being generated
outside that they're

00:22:24.070 --> 00:22:26.320
trying to bring in and do
the analysis on premise.

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:29.200
So a good example of how
we're working with this

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:32.890
ecosystem to certify and support
these enterprise applications

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:35.907
on top of the joint solution
between Cisco and Google.

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:40.670
All right.

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:42.330
So kind of summarize
what we're doing

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:44.330
and what you'll see in
the next couple sessions.

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:46.050
One, we're integrating.

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:48.500
So some of the
complexity that we're not

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:50.930
going to go into here, but
on the Thursday session,

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:53.300
is what are we doing southbound?

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:55.100
Do you need persistent storage?

00:22:55.100 --> 00:22:57.980
So Cisco has already done the
integration with the HyperFlex

00:22:57.980 --> 00:23:00.590
platform to be able to have
persistent storage as well

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:03.557
as with third parties, so
that if you need to save data,

00:23:03.557 --> 00:23:05.390
you actually have access
to that saved data.

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:07.700
How do I connect into the
network, both physical

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:09.129
and container networking?

00:23:09.129 --> 00:23:10.670
That's built into
container platform.

00:23:10.670 --> 00:23:12.339
How do I tie into
the Management We're

00:23:12.339 --> 00:23:13.880
providing a management
interface that

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:15.830
has a holistic view of IT ops.

00:23:15.830 --> 00:23:17.480
And then still,
we're not changing.

00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:20.510
I've showed you kind of
Cisco's view of the CICD,

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:23.314
where maybe the continuous
integration is Jenkins.

00:23:23.314 --> 00:23:24.980
And then we're using
Cloud Center Deploy

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:26.510
as the container's deployment.

00:23:26.510 --> 00:23:29.529
But because I said I'm
given the developers

00:23:29.529 --> 00:23:31.820
a consistent Kubernetes
environment that they can still

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:33.860
access just like they
were familiar with,

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:35.990
if they have their
own CICD pipeline,

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:37.880
it's still 100%
compatible with this.

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:40.790
I think Google started talking
about a Google Cloud-based

00:23:40.790 --> 00:23:42.740
build environment would
also be able to talk

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:43.710
to this environment.

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:46.100
So we're not forcing
customers or development teams

00:23:46.100 --> 00:23:47.960
